The reason your Cloudflare Pages deployment still shows the old interface (with Terminals 4, 5, and 6) even after redeploying comes down to two main factors: Aggressive Edge Caching on Cloudflare and the Service Worker cache (sw.js) stored locally in your browser/tablets.

When you configure a Progressive Web App (PWA) with a Service Worker, browsers aggressively cache the old JavaScript bundles (index-*.js) and index files offline. Even if you redeploy on Cloudflare, the browser's Service Worker will keep serving the cached offline version until the cache version name is updated or manually unregistered.

Here is how to fix the deployment caching issue immediately, followed by the complete, fully verified code to ensure your navigation and Reports module render correctly.

Step 1: Force Cloudflare Pages & Browser Cache Clear
Clear Cloudflare Cache:

Go to your Cloudflare Dashboard 
→ Workers & Pages 
→ your jjb-systems project 
→ Caches.

Click Purge Everything. This forces Cloudflare's global edge network to drop the old cached build immediately.

Bump Service Worker Version (public/sw.js):

Open public/sw.js and change const CACHE_NAME = 'jjb-wms-v4-cache'; to const CACHE_NAME = 'jjb-wms-v4.5-cache';.

This forces the browser to discard the old offline cache and fetch the newly built files upon reloading.

Hard Refresh Your Browser:

On desktop: Press Ctrl + Shift + R (Windows) or Cmd + Shift + R (Mac).

On mobile/tablets: Clear browser site data for jjb-systems.pages.dev or open an fresh Incognito/Private window.

